{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/statusview at 968dda7146aaacf3db72fa465a35f1f51170515d, replacing ed586b25 after codex blocked 96e87d82. Three commits on current main de30e088, under promise fc6c8720 on request 099e4c83.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE THE BLOCKED HEAD. Text tested unicode.IsSpace before hostile, so a newline, carriage return, tab, U+2028 or U+2029 became an ordinary space. Codex called that safe but not sufficient, and was right: request 099e4c83 asks for structural line-breaking characters rendered visibly and unambiguously, and a folded newline is silent -- the reader cannot tell a line break from a space somebody typed, so a bounded line stops saying what the record contained. Text now tests hostile first. Only benign spacing folds; an ordinary space is still a space.\n\nThis is the second time in this lane I stopped at \"cannot move the cursor or break a line\" when the condition said visible and unambiguous. I asked codex whether folding-before-escaping was sound, and it had already answered before I asked.\n\nWHAT THE HEAD DOES, in full. statusview.Text folded whitespace and capped bytes and passed everything else through, so any actor could put an escape sequence, a bell, a bidi override or an invisible joiner into a request, report, artifact path or chat message and every reader's terminal would execute it. Text now escapes the C0 and C1 controls, DEL, the Unicode format characters, the line and paragraph separators and invalid UTF-8, at fixed widths by range: \\xNN below U+0100, \\uNNNN through U+FFFF, \\UNNNNNNNN above. The byte cap truncates on a token boundary so it cannot split an escape. Safe applies the same escapes without the one-line fold or the cap, so a long message keeps its length and exact spacing.\n\nCONDITIONS MET. Durable bytes and JSON meaning are preserved: nothing in the log changes, only what a bounded view shows. Every caller of the shared path is covered, verified rather than asserted -- a test walks every string reachable through all eight view builders from a projection whose every actor-controlled field is hostile. It found five callers nobody had neutralized: chat frame text, actor kind in both the status and orientation views, artifact row paths, the echoed path query, and the staleness path echo. Request conditions and three attempt reasons were passing through as well. All are closed.\n\nWHAT THIS COSTS, stated rather than buried. Every multi-paragraph request text in a bounded view now shows \\x0a where it used to show a space, so gs status output is noisier for a human reading it. I judged that an audit surface should not quietly normalise what it shows, but it is a real readability loss and a reviewer is entitled to weigh it differently.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE. Fifteen mutations, one per neutralization site plus both escape switches, the cap boundary, the newline class, the non-BMP width and the escape/fold ordering, each applied alone: all caught, each failing by name. Restoring the whitespace fold ahead of the escape fails all six structural cases, each reporting \"a b\" where the escape belongs.\n\nTHE GOLDEN IS UNCHANGED, AND THAT PROVES NOTHING HERE. internal/statusview/testdata/bounded-summary-golden.json is byte-identical to the version codex reviewed at f38c0b92, but its hostile fixture contains no structural whitespace, so this round's change cannot appear in it. The table test is the evidence for this change; the golden only shows that the earlier encoder rework did not reshape output. The 400-byte shrink from the first commit is still the part most worth an independent rebuild.\n\nGATES at this exact head: gofmt clean, go vet clean, go test ./... clean, go test ./internal/docset/... clean at 62.1s, working tree clean, branch request/terminal-control-neutralization pushed to origin.\n\nThe layer contract does not change, so docs/reference/architecture.md is untouched. This head also changes cmd/gs by one line; that path carries its own artifact.","body":{"commit":"968dda7146aaacf3db72fa465a35f1f51170515d","path":"internal/statusview"}}